WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.592
Question seventeen.

00:00:01.592 --> 00:00:07.541
Step one: macro scan. It asks how many colours sixteen-bit depth gives.

00:00:07.541 --> 00:00:11.312
One mark. Command words: “number of colours”.

00:00:11.312 --> 00:00:12.820
Step two: denoise.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:15.502
Useful, in yellow: sixteen bits.

00:00:15.502 --> 00:00:19.273
In cyan: “per pixel” and “number of colours”.

00:00:19.273 --> 00:00:23.462
Useless red herring: digital camera — kick it out.

00:00:23.462 --> 00:00:24.971
Step three: draft.

00:00:24.971 --> 00:00:27.903
Colours equal two to the bit depth.

00:00:27.903 --> 00:00:29.495
Two to the sixteen.

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:33.182
Sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six.

00:00:33.182 --> 00:00:34.607
Step four: P O E.

00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:38.378
A, sixteen: bit count taken as colours — cut.

00:00:38.378 --> 00:00:44.662
C, thirty-two thousand seven hundred sixty-eight: two to the fifteen — cut.

00:00:44.662 --> 00:00:48.768
D, two hundred fifty-six: two to the eight — cut.

00:00:48.768 --> 00:00:56.226
Step five: sanity check and lock. Sixteen bits gives two to the sixteen — the value fits.

00:00:56.226 --> 00:00:58.488
So the correct answer is B.
